## Why This Is Asked
Tests incremental connectivity intuition, DSU basics, and amortized analysis for real-world batch updates.

## Key Concepts
- Union-Find with path compression and union by rank
- Alpha(n)
- Batch vs online update trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
class DSU {
  constructor(n){
    this.parent = Array.from({length:n}, (_,i)=>i);
    this.rank = new Array(n).fill(0);
    this.components = n;
  }
  find(x){ return this.parent[x]===x? x: this.find(this.parent[x]); }
  union(a,b){ let ra=this.find(a), rb=this.find(b); if(ra===rb) return false; if(this.rank[ra]<this.rank[rb])[ra,rb]=[rb,ra]; this.parent[rb]=ra; if(this.rank[ra]===this.rank[rb]) this.rank[ra]++; this.components--; return true; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for directed graphs and counting weak components?
- What heuristics decide when to trigger a rebuild versus continuing online?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests incremental triangle counting under streaming edge additions; evaluates ability to design batched analysis and compare to full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental graph algorithms
- Degree-based edge orientation
- Wedge counting and triangle updates
- Amortized analysis
- Space-time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function addEdge(u,v,adj,deg,tri){
  if (deg[u] > deg[v]) [u,v] = [v,u];
  let cnt = 0;
  for (const w of adj[u]){
    if (adj[v].has(w)) cnt++;
  }
  tri.total += cnt;
  adj[u].add(v); adj[v].add(u);
  deg[u]++; deg[v]++;
}
```

## Further Discussion
This batched approach significantly outperforms rebuilding from scratch, which would require O(n³) time in the worst case for triangle enumeration. The incremental method achieves near-linear preprocessing and maintains efficient updates, making it practical for large-scale dynamic graphs where edges arrive continuously.

